WEBVTT
TITLE Pointers in C — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
A variable is a name for a place in memory.

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:11.000
int x = 42 reserves four bytes. The first byte's address is the address of x.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.000
So x has two separate things: an address, 1000, and a value, 42.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
An ordinary variable keeps the value. A pointer keeps the address.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.000
The ampersand means "address of". So &x is 1000.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:29.000
int *p = &x; declares p as a pointer to int, and stores 1000 in it.

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.000
p is a variable too. It lives at its own address and takes eight bytes here.

8
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.000
A pointer is a variable whose value is an address.

9
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.000
The star in an expression means "the object at the address stored here".

10
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Follow the arrow: p holds 1000, and 1000 is where x lives. So *p is x itself.

11
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
*p = 99 writes through the arrow. x becomes 99. p does not change.

12
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
That is why &*p is p and *&x is x: the two operators undo each other.

13
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Pointer arithmetic is scaled by the size of the type pointed to.

14
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.000
On an int pointer, p + 1 moves four bytes, not one.

15
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:17.000
On a char pointer, q + 1 moves one byte, because sizeof(char) is 1 by definition.

16
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:24.000
addr(p + k) = addr(p) + k x sizeof(*p). One rule, both rows.

17
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
An array is a block of equal-sized cells, laid out one after another.

18
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
In most expressions the name a decays: it becomes &a[0], a pointer to the first element.

19
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:43.000
a and &a[0] hold the same address, but they are not the same type.

20
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.000
a[i] is defined as *(a + i). So a[2] and *(a + 2) reach the same cell.

21
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Since addition commutes, 2[a] is legal and means the same cell.

22
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.000
sizeof asks about the object. It answers in bytes, and it is computed from the type.

23
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:04.000
sizeof(a) is 20: five ints of four bytes. The array knows its own size.

24
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.000
sizeof(p) is 8: the pointer's own size, whatever it points at.

25
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.000
&a is a pointer to the whole array, so &a + 1 jumps twenty bytes, past all of it.

26
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Inside a function, an array parameter has already decayed, so sizeof gives 8, not 20.

27
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.000
The star and the increment can combine in four ways, and each means something different.

28
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:34.000
*p++ is *(p++): read the current cell, then move p forward.

29
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:41.000
(*p)++ increments the cell and leaves p where it is.

30
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:48.000
++*p increments the cell before reading it; *++p moves first, then reads.

31
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Postfix binds tighter than star. Brackets decide everything else.

32
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:02.000
A pointer variable has an address too, so a pointer can point at it.

33
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:09.000
int **pp = &p stores the address of p. **pp follows two arrows to reach 7.

34
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:18.000
This is how a function changes the caller's pointer, not just the value it points at.

35
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
A local variable lives only while its function is running.

36
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Returning its address gives a pointer to a frame that no longer exists.

37
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.000
The pointer still holds the number. The object is gone. That is a dangling pointer.

38
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Reading or writing through it is undefined behaviour, not a predictable value.

39
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Rule one: p + k moves k elements, never k bytes.

40
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Rule two: a[i] is *(a + i), and the name decays except under sizeof and &.

41
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Rule three: sizeof reports the object's size; a pointer reports only its own.

